A||||5||Homicidal||Subwoofer Placement Problem: Bass Guys Respond PLEASE!||||8||
Z||000000||Homicidal||09-12-2000||01:12 AM||||Posted on both Forums since I couldn't decide it "General" or "Technical".<P>I currently have my 10" Polk db Series sub behind my drivers seat, and I really like the sound, but the cramped space it getting hard to deal with.<P>So I wanna move it, without losing TOO much quality.  I currently have a 12 cd changer behind the passenger side seat, and I don't like the footwell idea AT ALL.<P>So what are my alternatives?  The front trunk sounds REALLY nice, but I need a PERFECT box for it.  What size dimensions do I need for the box?  Does anybody have pics?<P>I also like this idea:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/1930/subwoof.jpg">  <P>But how do I pull it off?  What size box?<P>Any help is appreciated!<P>BTW, I found a small box at work that has the dimensions of L 20" X W 8" X H 16", would this fit in the front compartment?  It's 8" Width on the bottom and is 6" on the top, so it's a slanted box, not a perfect square.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Bugsy||09-12-2000||06:21 AM||||I've got a Kenwood 200 watt amp driving a Sony Xplode 10" sub behind the passenger seat.  The changer is in the space behind the engine compartment. I can take some pics if you'd like.<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||6
Z||000002||overlord||09-12-2000||08:48 AM||||i had a sub behind each seat (cheap ones), upgraded to 1 better sub (small enclosure) and i didnt like it when they were behind the seats. Rattled everything in the back and the bass wasnt very good.  So i built a box for the footwell.  WOW! sounds sooo much better. Can actually hear my car thumping from half mile away now.  If you dont mind losing a bit of leg room for the passenger then id say that is the best way to do it.<P>My box is entirley made from 3/4 MDF and is about .86 cubic feet. I'm 6 foot+ and i can ride in passenger seat fine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||09-12-2000||05:34 PM||||I made my own. I took a bass cannon and cut it to about 6 1/2 inches long and mounted it in the stock location. It has a 100 watt amp that came with the cannon and I put it in the stock amp location. It has good hard hitting bass. I want to add a cross tube like the stock one on the top to see if it gets even more bass because it doesn't hit as hard with the roof off and the windows down.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jul18^04.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Jul18^07.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000004||overlord||09-12-2000||07:04 PM||||thefiero64..<P>that wouldnt happen to be a "sparkomatic"<BR>bass cannon eh?<P>if it is i used to have  a couple in my old sunbird GT.......cant get em anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Boomtastic||09-13-2000||12:14 AM||||Since there's no substitute for speaker size, put the largest size speaker you can behind the passenger seat. I opted for a Lanzar LP12 Dual Voicecoil, crossed at 80Hz, ported at 40Hz. Power is supplied by a Rockford Fosgate Punch 150 in a stereo-mono mode.<P>If I can ever get PIP to work, I'd show off the speaker. Until then, visit my webpage if you'd like a peek. <A HREF="http://www.boomtastic.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.boomtastic.com</A> <P>||||reg||12
